 - 2026-11-20, about 08:23 UTC: **the shared map and London** (Dinis, 19 Nov about 17:40 UTC, board/guidance/2026-11-19-cameras-as-links-and-the-map.md; ptmap by designer@, review passed by security@ 168c14fc5, listed by dev@ a81b20325). designer@'s ptmap 1.0.2 is vendored in src/assets/ptmap/1.0.2/ (from PrompTugal__News__Website 758810f86; ptmap's code Apache-2.0, the outline Natural Earth via world-atlas, whose ISC licence stays in the file). The home page has a "Where" section: mainland Portugal with a small London inset linking to the new /london/ page. It shows no dots, because every figure here is worldwide, and no EU inset while EUVD is paused. /london/ says what this site has for London: worldwide totals only, and the UK NCSC's advisories on data.b@'s register, not read. No pin is kept in the repository (the leak scan reads SRI values as possible secrets): each build checks both files against the registry's pins and review, and leaves the map out when the registry cannot be read, the version is dropped or its review does not pass; a different pin stops the build. 11 test cases (scripts/test-ptmap.mjs), run by release.sh and CI.
 - 2026-11-20, about 19:20 UTC: **the shared map's wiring tested, and the data refreshed** (010d1db: pulse, CVE store with 8 new versions, preview, from data.b@'s 18:04 and 18:15 UTC reads). scripts/test-ptmap.mjs now also runs the whole build in a throwaway copy with fetch stubbed: with no registry the home page carries no map tags and says the map is off; with matching pins it carries them, the map script before the init script; /london/ has its CSP. 14 cases. ENISA's publications (enisa-pubs) stay out of this site until a person has read ENISA's legal notice (rights@ R10), as for EUVD.
 - 2026-11-21, about 08:23 UTC: **a proposed mapping of products to sectors** (prompted by Dinis's answer of 18 Nov about 23:41 UTC, "I think that sounds quite good"; not approved by him). /sectors/ and /api/v1/sectors/ (mapping.json, the rules; index.json, the counts) class each CVE on CISA KEV now by the kind of product it concerns: industrial and building control (5 product rules, each with NVD product names as evidence) or general-purpose IT (32 vendors). Of 1,790 CVEs (KEV read 2026-11-21 07:04 UTC): general IT 1,201 (67.1%), industrial or building control 5, not classed yet 584 (32.6%). Shown as typical use, not exposure, never summed into sectors, and nothing about Portugal's infrastructure. scripts/sectors.mjs checks the mapping (status, fields, classes, duplicates, stale rules) and a fault stops the build; 12 test cases (scripts/test-sectors.mjs), run by release.sh and CI. Data refreshed the same wake (8850677).
